<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
  xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-3.8.xsd">

  <!--
    DH-011G2 (LIBAN DECISION: SELECT OPTION D, patches/order-correlation/PROVENANCE.md): the
    Daher-owned native linkage between an OpenMRS Patient UUID (the source-system identity) and an
    OpenELIS Patient. DH-011G's own investigation (this file's sibling PROVENANCE.md section)
    proved two things that rule out every alternative: (1) OpenELIS's FHIR Patient ingestion
    recognizes exactly four hardcoded identifier systems and silently drops anything else, so a
    generic external identifier cannot be recognized without either abusing one of those four
    buckets (rejected; see the DECISIONS.md ADR: pat_nationalId etc. keep their own native
    meanings) or a dedicated table; (2) Patient.id/pat_guid/pat_uuid are always server-generated on
    create, so the OpenMRS UUID cannot be honored as OpenELIS's own identity either. This table is
    that dedicated mechanism.

    Deliberately generic on identifier_system/identifier_value (not a scalar "omrs_uuid" column):
    the V1 Java code (PatientProvider.java.patch) only ever writes and reads exactly one system,
    DaherPatientIdentity.SYSTEM = 'urn:daher-health:openmrs-patient-uuid', enforced entirely at the
    application layer, the same division of responsibility DaherOrderCorrelation.SYSTEM already
    uses for ServiceRequest. Keeping the column generic avoids a second migration if a future,
    explicitly-decided second source system is ever added; nothing here weakens V1's own single-
    system contract, which is a Java-level invariant, not a schema one.

    Columns beyond the ticket's stated minimum (id, patient_id, identifier_system,
    identifier_value, created_at): last_updated is required by
    org.openelisglobal.common.valueholder.BaseObject, exactly as documented in
    004-daher-analysis-correlation.xml: every entity reachable through this codebase's shared
    BaseDAO/BaseObjectService infrastructure extends it and it unconditionally maps a @Version
    column named last_updated. This table is insert-only in V1 (no UPDATE is ever issued against
    it; a Patient's demographics can change without touching its identity link, and the link
    itself is never repointed), so optimistic-lock conflicts on it are not expected in practice,
    but the column must exist for the entity to load through that shared infrastructure at all.

    Constraints:
      - patient_id: NOT NULL, FK to clinlims.patient(id) (NUMERIC(10,0), matching Patient.hbm.xml's
        own id mapping via LIMSStringNumberUserType; see DaherPatientIdentity.java's javadoc for
        why the Java side maps this as Long, not the DB-adjacent String type). No ON DELETE action
        specified (the default, NO ACTION): OpenELIS's own PatientProvider.delete does not hard-
        delete the native Patient row (it only syncs an inactive flag to the secondary FHIR store),
        so this migration must not silently lose linkage/audit evidence by cascading a delete that
        upstream itself does not perform.
      - identifier_value: NOT NULL, lowercase-hex-with-hyphens UUID format (matching the exact
        lowercase form java.util.UUID.randomUUID().toString() and OpenMRS's own UUID generation
        produce): a defense-in-depth check, mirroring 004's own correlation_value format
        constraint, so a malformed value fails at insert time (a real Postgres constraint
        violation, surfaced loudly) rather than being silently accepted and later read back as an
        opaque, unvalidated string.
      - UNIQUE(identifier_system, identifier_value) (DH-011G2 §14, first case): the same OpenMRS
        UUID can never be linked to two different OpenELIS Patients, enforced at the database,
        not merely by the adapter's own search-before-create discipline, which DH-011G's live
        duplicate-Patient proof already showed OpenELIS will not do for you on its own.
      - UNIQUE(patient_id, identifier_system) (DH-011G2 §5/§14, second case; LIBAN DECISION:
        ENFORCE): one OpenELIS Patient can never carry two different OpenMRS UUIDs under the Daher
        system. Chosen deliberately, not left as the ticket's own "evaluate" default, because the
        alternative (allowing it) is exactly the mechanism that would let two distinct OpenMRS
        patients accidentally collapse onto one lab identity, the single failure mode this whole
        ticket exists to close. Together with the first constraint, this makes (patient_id,
        identifier_value) a true one-to-one mapping for V1's one identifier_system.

    Fails safely, not destructively: if this table or its objects already exist under a name this
    migration collides with, CREATE fails and Liquibase does not mark the changeset applied. No
    existing clinlims.patient row is read, rewritten, or backfilled: there is no historical
    OpenMRS-UUID linkage to reconstruct for a patient created before this migration, and this
    migration does not attempt to invent one (see DH-011G2 §18 for why auto-linking existing,
    unlinked patients by demographic similarity is explicitly out of scope).
  -->
  <changeSet id="daher-011g2-006-daher-patient-identity" author="daher-health">
    <sql>
      CREATE SEQUENCE daher_patient_identity_seq;

      CREATE TABLE daher_patient_identity (
        id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT nextval('daher_patient_identity_seq'),
        patient_id NUMERIC(10,0) NOT NULL
          REFERENCES clinlims.patient (id),
        identifier_system VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
        identifier_value VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
          CONSTRAINT daher_patient_identity_value_format
          CHECK (identifier_value ~ '^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}$'),
        created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
        last_updated TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
      );

      ALTER SEQUENCE daher_patient_identity_seq OWNED BY daher_patient_identity.id;

      CREATE UNIQUE INDEX daher_patient_identity_system_value_uk
        ON daher_patient_identity (identifier_system, identifier_value);

      CREATE UNIQUE INDEX daher_patient_identity_patient_system_uk
        ON daher_patient_identity (patient_id, identifier_system);
    </sql>
    <rollback>
      <sql>
        DROP TABLE IF EXISTS daher_patient_identity;
        DROP SEQUENCE IF EXISTS daher_patient_identity_seq;
      </sql>
    </rollback>
  </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
